  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B044",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and realization of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B034",
    "B010"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "I change my preferred configuration from B034 to B044. KPZ remains an outstanding conceptual achievement, but the unresolved breadth of its experimental support makes a prize for the full universality class less securely delimited than I would want at the voting stage. Optical lattice clocks instead join a precise physical principle—magic-wavelength confinement—with a mature, independently reproducible realization and a clearly demonstrated new capacity for measurement. Katori and Ye represent complementary conceptual and experimental contributions in a compact two-person allocation. The citation must remain restricted to the lattice-clock architecture, not optical-frequency metrology in general.",
    "case_against": "B044 risks being seen chiefly as an exceptionally successful metrological technology rather than a discovery of a universal physical mechanism. Its historical boundary also remains essential: the committee must ensure that Katori and Ye can be credited for the originating lattice-clock invention and realization without absorbing earlier optical-clock or frequency-metrology contributions. If that attribution cannot be sustained, the apparent clarity of the two-person configuration would be illusory.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that optical lattice clocks combine a decisive physical idea, mature reproducibility, and a coherent attribution to Katori and Ye, while insisting on separation from broader optical-clock development.",
        "response": "I find that delimitation decisive. The magic-wavelength lattice architecture is not simply another performance increment: it provides a controllable many-atom route to exceptional precision. Kroll is right, however, that this argument holds only if the historical review keeps the citation at that architectural innovation rather than treating it as credit for precision timekeeping as a whole."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson prefers aberration-corrected electron optics because it made reliable sub-angstrom microscopy routine, but identifies a serious attribution question spanning theory, practical instruments, and broader engineering contributions.",
        "response": "I agree that B010 represents a deep and durable transformation of experimental physics, and it remains my leading alternative. The comparison has moved me toward B044 because the latter presently offers a more tightly identifiable mechanism and a less distributed proposed allocation. That is a credit judgment, not a claim that optical clocks are scientifically more valuable merely because they have fewer names."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson defends the quantum spin Hall effect as a mature theory-experiment achievement but recognizes the need to avoid overlap with prior recognition of topological phases.",
        "response": "The Kane-Mele and Molenkamp configuration has real conceptual force. I remain concerned that the necessary narrow citation may still sit too close to a broader already recognized topological-phase domain. B044 has a comparably specific physical core and a more readily bounded prize object, provided its own metrological lineage is treated with equal historical care."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether documentary priority review confirms that Katori and Ye alone are the historically proportionate recipients for the magic-wavelength lattice-clock invention and realization.",
      "Whether the final citation can sharply exclude broader optical-frequency metrology and later clock-performance refinements.",
      "Whether the evidence for KPZ warrants a future citation framed around the full universality class rather than kinetic roughening."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
